-- 1. the default policy
ok 1.1 the whole policy, in a fixed directive order
ok 1.2 scripts may load from the page's own origin
ok 1.3 and the socket may open to it
ok 1.4 no 'unsafe-inline'
ok 1.5 no 'unsafe-eval'
ok 1.6 and framing is refused outright

-- 2. espresso's security_headers, run and read
ok 2.1 espresso still sends the policy we measured
ok 2.2 it names no script source at all
ok 2.3 and no connect source
ok 2.4 so every fetch falls back to a default that refuses everything

-- 3. the four headers, through the real pipeline
ok 3.1 the page is served
ok 3.2 the policy
ok 3.3 nosniff
ok 3.4 the older framing header goes out beside frame-ancestors
ok 3.5 and a referrer policy
ok 3.6 a 404 is the pipeline's own answer
ok 3.7 and it carries the policy too

-- 3b. a handler's own policy is not overwritten
ok 3.8 the handler's policy stands
ok 3.9 and the other three still go out

-- 4. js/latte.js needs no 'unsafe-inline' and no 'unsafe-eval'
ok 4.0 js/latte.js is readable
ok 4.1 no eval(
ok 4.2 no new Function(
ok 4.3 no dynamic import()
ok 4.4 no setTimeout with a string body
ok 4.5 no document.write
ok 4.6 no javascript: URL
ok 4.7 it opens a WebSocket, which is why connect-src is named
ok 4.8 and an XMLHttpRequest for enhanced navigation

-- 5. the options are the escape, and they are scoped
ok 5.1 both lists land in their own directive
ok 5.2 a page that IS meant to be framed says so in the policy
ok 5.3 the legacy header stays DENY, having no way to say it
ok 5.4 and the referrer policy is the one asked for

